<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chicago Boys</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chicago Boys were Chilean economists trained at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a>, many through an exchange programme with the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chile">University of Chile</a> begun in the 1950s. From the mid-1970s they directed economic policy under the military regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet">Augusto Pinochet</a>, cutting tariffs, privatising state firms and the pension system and promoting exports. Their reforms became an early model of free-market policy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chicago-boys.jpg" alt="Chicago Boys" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jorge Barrios · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peso_Chileno_1933.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The group's training grew out of the Chile Project, organised in the 1950s by the United States State Department under the Point Four Program and funded by the Ford Foundation and the Rockefeller Foundation, with the aim of reshaping Chilean economic thought. Between 1955 and 1973, one hundred twenty-six selected Chilean students were trained first in Chile and then in postgraduate studies at Chicago, under teachers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-friedman">Milton Friedman</a>, Arnold Harberger and Larry Sjaastad. The first wave, from 1955 to 1964, was set up by the United States Agency for International Development, and the second, from 1965 to 1973, was funded by the Ford Foundation. The name Chicago Boys itself emerged in the 1980s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Their ideas remained marginal in Chilean politics until the early 1970s. A group of them wrote a 189-page economic programme, El ladrillo (the brick), for Jorge Alessandri's unsuccessful presidential campaign of 1969; Alessandri rejected it, but it was revived after the 1973 coup that brought General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet">Augusto Pinochet</a> to power. In 1975, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> still high and a world recession pushing Chile into depression, Pinochet appointed several of them to powerful posts. Members included the finance ministers Jorge Cauas, Sergio de Castro and Hernán Büchi, and José Piñera, minister of labour and pensions.</p><p>Their programme, often called shock therapy, combined severe austerity, deep cuts in government spending, deregulation, privatisation of state-owned enterprises and the dismantling of protectionist barriers to trade. By 1982 Chile's GDP had contracted by about 15 per cent, and the reforms widened income inequality. The junta presented them as a corrective to the socialist policies before it and a bulwark against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Chilean Miracle, as supporters called the results, gave the regime a degree of international legitimacy that helped offset criticism of its human rights record, and it influenced the turn to free-market policy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> in the United Kingdom. Critics point to the social costs and to the policies being imposed under a repressive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>. After the return to democracy in 1990 the group lost direct political power, though the core of its economic model remained in place, and Chicago graduates also shaped policy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chicago Boys” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 1970s the military regime's economists, known as the <b>Chicago Boys</b>, opened the economy, privatised state firms and pensions and promoted exports of fruit, wine, timber and fish.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
